1
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:09,679
Casting my eyes on Mr. Wemmick as we went along, to see what he was like in the light of day,

2
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:16,199
I found him to be a dry man, rather short in stature, with a square wooden face,

3
00:00:16,199 --> 00:00:21,679
whose expression seemed to have been imperfectly chipped out with a dull-edged chisel. There were

4
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:26,319
some marks in it that might have been dimples, if the material had been softer and the instrument

5
00:00:26,319 --> 00:00:33,438
finer, but which, as it was, were only dints. The chisel had made three or four of these

6
00:00:33,438 --> 00:00:38,039
attempts at embellishment over his nose, but had given them up without an effort to smooth

7
00:00:38,039 --> 00:00:44,039
them off. I judged him to be a bachelor from the frayed condition of his linen, and he appeared

8
00:00:44,039 --> 00:00:49,798
to have sustained a good many bereavements; for he wore at least four mourning rings,

9
00:00:49,798 --> 00:00:56,520
besides a brooch representing a lady and a weeping willow at a tomb with an urn on it. I noticed,

10
00:00:56,520 --> 00:01:01,638
too, that several rings and seals hung at his watch-chain, as if he were quite laden

11
00:01:01,638 --> 00:01:09,240
with remembrances of departed friends. He had glittering eyes,—small, keen, and black,—and

12
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:17,159
thin wide mottled lips. He had had them, to the best of my belief, from forty to fifty years.

13
00:01:17,159 --> 00:01:21,319
“So you were never in London before?” said Mr. Wemmick to me.

14
00:01:21,319 --> 00:01:23,000
“No,” said I.

15
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,118
“I was new here once,” said Mr. Wemmick. “Rum to think of now!”

16
00:01:28,118 --> 00:01:30,319
“You are well acquainted with it now?”

17
00:01:30,319 --> 00:01:33,958
“Why, yes,” said Mr. Wemmick. “I know the moves of it.”

18
00:01:33,958 --> 00:01:40,680
“Is it a very wicked place?” I asked, more for the sake of saying something than for information.

19
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:44,638
“You may get cheated, robbed, and murdered in London. But there are

20
00:01:44,638 --> 00:01:48,319
plenty of people anywhere, who’ll do that for you.”

21
00:01:48,319 --> 00:01:54,359
“If there is bad blood between you and them,” said I, to soften it off a little.

22
00:01:54,359 --> 00:01:58,080
“O! I don’t know about bad blood,” returned Mr. Wemmick;

23
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:03,438
“there’s not much bad blood about. They’ll do it, if there’s anything to be got by it.”

24
00:02:03,438 --> 00:02:05,558
“That makes it worse.”

25
00:02:05,559 --> 00:02:11,520
“You think so?” returned Mr. Wemmick. “Much about the same, I should say.”

26
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:15,520
He wore his hat on the back of his head, and looked straight before him:

27
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:19,319
walking in a self-contained way as if there were nothing in the streets to

28
00:02:19,319 --> 00:02:24,118
claim his attention. His mouth was such a post-office of a mouth that he had a

29
00:02:24,118 --> 00:02:30,240
mechanical appearance of smiling. We had got to the top of Holborn Hill before I knew that it

30
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:35,840
was merely a mechanical appearance, and that he was not smiling at all.

31
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:40,520
“Do you know where Mr. Matthew Pocket lives?” I asked Mr. Wemmick.

32
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:44,360
“Yes,” said he, nodding in the direction. “At Hammersmith, west of London.”

33
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:46,840
“Is that far?”

34
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:49,240
“Well! Say five miles.”

35
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:50,840
“Do you know him?”

36
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:54,759
“Why, you’re a regular cross-examiner!” said Mr. Wemmick, looking at me with an

37
00:02:54,759 --> 00:02:58,919
approving air. “Yes, I know him. I know him!”

38
00:02:58,919 --> 00:03:03,520
There was an air of toleration or depreciation about his utterance of these words that rather

39
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:09,039
depressed me; and I was still looking sideways at his block of a face in search of any encouraging

40
00:03:09,039 --> 00:03:16,879
note to the text, when he said here we were at Barnard’s Inn. My depression was not alleviated

41
00:03:16,879 --> 00:03:22,400
by the announcement, for, I had supposed that establishment to be an hotel kept by Mr. Barnard,

42
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:27,558
to which the Blue Boar in our town was a mere public-house. Whereas I now found Barnard to

43
00:03:27,559 --> 00:03:32,599
be a disembodied spirit, or a fiction, and his inn the dingiest collection of

44
00:03:32,599 --> 00:03:38,559
shabby buildings ever squeezed together in a rank corner as a club for Tom-cats.

45
00:03:38,559 --> 00:03:43,759
We entered this haven through a wicket-gate, and were disgorged by an introductory passage into

46
00:03:43,759 --> 00:03:49,000
a melancholy little square that looked to me like a flat burying-ground. I thought it had

47
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:54,559
the most dismal trees in it, and the most dismal sparrows, and the most dismal cats,

48
00:03:54,559 --> 00:04:00,840
and the most dismal houses (in number half a dozen or so), that I had ever seen. I thought

49
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:05,520
the windows of the sets of chambers into which those houses were divided were in every stage

50
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:12,478
of dilapidated blind and curtain, crippled flower-pot, cracked glass, dusty decay,

51
00:04:12,478 --> 00:04:19,319
and miserable makeshift; while To Let, To Let, To Let, glared at me from empty rooms,

52
00:04:19,319 --> 00:04:24,558
as if no new wretches ever came there, and the vengeance of the soul of Barnard were

53
00:04:24,559 --> 00:04:30,840
being slowly appeased by the gradual suicide of the present occupants and their unholy interment

54
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:38,159
under the gravel. A frowzy mourning of soot and smoke attired this forlorn creation of Barnard,

55
00:04:38,159 --> 00:04:43,840
and it had strewn ashes on its head, and was undergoing penance and humiliation as a mere

56
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:51,599
dust-hole. Thus far my sense of sight; while dry rot and wet rot and all the silent rots that rot

57
00:04:51,599 --> 00:04:57,199
in neglected roof and cellar,—rot of rat and mouse and bug and coaching-stables near at hand

58
00:04:57,199 --> 00:05:04,919
besides—addressed themselves faintly to my sense of smell, and moaned, “Try Barnard’s Mixture.”

59
00:05:04,919 --> 00:05:09,240
So imperfect was this realisation of the first of my great expectations,

60
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:16,477
that I looked in dismay at Mr. Wemmick. “Ah!” said he, mistaking me; “the retirement reminds

61
00:05:16,478 --> 00:05:22,520
you of the country. So it does me.” He led me into a corner and conducted

62
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:28,080
me up a flight of stairs,—which appeared to me to be slowly collapsing into sawdust,

63
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:32,638
so that one of those days the upper lodgers would look out at their doors and find

64
00:05:32,638 --> 00:05:40,278
themselves without the means of coming down,—to a set of chambers on the top floor. MR. POCKET,

65
00:05:40,278 --> 00:05:47,558
JUN., was painted on the door, and there was a label on the letter-box, “Return shortly.”

66
00:05:47,559 --> 00:05:53,919
“He hardly thought you’d come so soon,” Mr. Wemmick explained. “You don’t want me any more?”

67
00:05:53,919 --> 00:05:56,039
“No, thank you,” said I.

68
00:05:56,039 --> 00:06:03,400
“As I keep the cash,” Mr. Wemmick observed, “we shall most likely meet pretty often. Good day.”

69
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:05,438
“Good day.”

70
00:06:05,438 --> 00:06:08,637
I put out my hand, and Mr. Wemmick at first looked at it as if he thought I

71
00:06:08,638 --> 00:06:13,638
wanted something. Then he looked at me, and said, correcting himself,—

72
00:06:13,638 --> 00:06:19,719
“To be sure! Yes. You’re in the habit of shaking hands?”

73
00:06:19,719 --> 00:06:25,680
I was rather confused, thinking it must be out of the London fashion, but said yes.

74
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:31,199
“I have got so out of it!” said Mr. Wemmick,—“except at last. Very glad,

75
00:06:31,199 --> 00:06:35,798
I’m sure, to make your acquaintance. Good day!”

76
00:06:35,798 --> 00:06:40,000
When we had shaken hands and he was gone, I opened the staircase window and had nearly

77
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,240
beheaded myself, for, the lines had rotted away, and it came down like

78
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:51,598
the guillotine. Happily it was so quick that I had not put my head out. After this escape,

79
00:06:51,599 --> 00:06:56,879
I was content to take a foggy view of the Inn through the window’s encrusting dirt,

80
00:06:56,879 --> 00:07:04,039
and to stand dolefully looking out, saying to myself that London was decidedly overrated.

81
00:07:04,039 --> 00:07:09,759
Mr. Pocket, Junior’s, idea of Shortly was not mine, for I had nearly maddened myself with

82
00:07:09,759 --> 00:07:15,118
looking out for half an hour, and had written my name with my finger several times in the dirt of

83
00:07:15,118 --> 00:07:22,558
every pane in the window, before I heard footsteps on the stairs. Gradually there arose before me the

84
00:07:22,559 --> 00:07:30,919
hat, head, neckcloth, waistcoat, trousers, boots, of a member of society of about my own standing.

85
00:07:30,919 --> 00:07:36,840
He had a paper-bag under each arm and a pottle of strawberries in one hand, and was out of breath.

86
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:38,879
“Mr. Pip?” said he.

87
00:07:38,879 --> 00:07:40,798
“Mr. Pocket?” said I.

88
00:07:40,798 --> 00:07:45,438
“Dear me!” he exclaimed. “I am extremely sorry; but I knew there was a coach from

89
00:07:45,438 --> 00:07:50,919
your part of the country at midday, and I thought you would come by that one. The fact is, I have

90
00:07:50,919 --> 00:07:56,278
been out on your account,—not that that is any excuse,—for I thought, coming from the country,

91
00:07:56,278 --> 00:08:02,400
you might like a little fruit after dinner, and I went to Covent Garden Market to get it good.”

92
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:07,879
For a reason that I had, I felt as if my eyes would start out of my head. I

93
00:08:07,879 --> 00:08:12,759
acknowledged his attention incoherently, and began to think this was a dream.

94
00:08:12,759 --> 00:08:17,478
“Dear me!” said Mr. Pocket, Junior. “This door sticks so!”

95
00:08:17,478 --> 00:08:21,599
As he was fast making jam of his fruit by wrestling with the door while the paper-bags

96
00:08:21,599 --> 00:08:27,118
were under his arms, I begged him to allow me to hold them. He relinquished them with an agreeable

97
00:08:27,118 --> 00:08:33,759
smile, and combated with the door as if it were a wild beast. It yielded so suddenly at last,

98
00:08:33,759 --> 00:08:39,599
that he staggered back upon me, and I staggered back upon the opposite door, and we both laughed.

99
00:08:39,599 --> 00:08:46,478
But still I felt as if my eyes must start out of my head, and as if this must be a dream.

100
00:08:46,478 --> 00:08:53,199
“Pray come in,” said Mr. Pocket, Junior. “Allow me to lead the way. I am rather bare here, but I hope

101
00:08:53,199 --> 00:08:58,477
you’ll be able to make out tolerably well till Monday. My father thought you would get on more

102
00:08:58,478 --> 00:09:04,118
agreeably through to-morrow with me than with him, and might like to take a walk about London. I am

103
00:09:04,118 --> 00:09:10,438
sure I shall be very happy to show London to you. As to our table, you won’t find that bad, I hope,

104
00:09:10,438 --> 00:09:15,080
for it will be supplied from our coffee-house here, and (it is only right I should add) at

105
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:22,719
your expense, such being Mr. Jaggers’s directions. As to our lodging, it’s not by any means splendid,

106
00:09:22,719 --> 00:09:26,599
because I have my own bread to earn, and my father hasn’t anything to give me,

107
00:09:26,599 --> 00:09:32,559
and I shouldn’t be willing to take it, if he had. This is our sitting-room,—just such chairs

108
00:09:32,559 --> 00:09:38,478
and tables and carpet and so forth, you see, as they could spare from home. You mustn’t give me

109
00:09:38,478 --> 00:09:43,919
credit for the tablecloth and spoons and castors, because they come for you from the coffee-house.

110
00:09:44,438 --> 00:09:50,679
This is my little bedroom; rather musty, but Barnard’s is musty. This is your bedroom;

111
00:09:50,680 --> 00:09:55,478
the furniture’s hired for the occasion, but I trust it will answer the purpose;

112
00:09:55,478 --> 00:10:00,118
if you should want anything, I’ll go and fetch it. The chambers are retired,

113
00:10:00,118 --> 00:10:07,080
and we shall be alone together, but we shan’t fight, I dare say. But dear me, I beg your pardon,

114
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:13,840
you’re holding the fruit all this time. Pray let me take these bags from you. I am quite ashamed.”

115
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:21,000
As I stood opposite to Mr. Pocket, Junior, delivering him the bags, One, Two, I saw the

116
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:27,879
starting appearance come into his own eyes that I knew to be in mine, and he said, falling back,—

117
00:10:27,879 --> 00:10:31,478
“Lord bless me, you’re the prowling boy!”

118
00:10:31,478 --> 00:10:36,000
“And you,” said I, “are the pale young gentleman!” Chapter 22.
